  "result": "I would sever the link between identity and being right\u2014so that realizing you are mistaken feels like putting down a heavy bag instead of losing a fight.\n\nMost of the world's avoidable damage doesn't come from pure malice. It comes from people defending bad ideas, unjust systems, and unnecessary grudges long after the evidence has turned, simply because admitting error feels like a threat to their worth or their place in a group. Once a belief becomes part of who someone is, truth stops being information and starts feeling like an attack.\n\nIf that reflex were gone, propaganda would lose most of its grip, leaders wouldn't drag out disasters just to save face, and disagreements wouldn't automatically harden into tribal warfare. People would still disagree and still want different things, and material problems would still exist\u2014but humanity would actually be able to self-correct at the speed of what it learns, instead of waiting for generations to die off so old mistakes can quietly retire.",
